        'en' => [
            'title' => 'Of 28,295,982 adults with diabetes or hypertension, only 21.0% had a UACR.',
            'image_alt' => 'A glass plate with a straight teal line in the foreground, and a translucent purple kidney with a darker curved channel behind it.',
            'excerpt' => 'On 19 October 2026 a Nefro-projekt review came out on longevity medicine and what “normal” does not mean for the kidneys. In a U.S. laboratory database from 2013 to 2019, 89.6% of at-risk adults had an eGFR and 21.0% had a UACR. Another panel does not replace that.',
            'content' => <<<'HTML'
<p>On 19 October 2026 I published a review on the Nefro-projekt site, <a href="https://nefro.polascin.net/article.php?slug=longevity-normalne-vysledky-cielene-testovanie-ckd"><em>Keď sú výsledky „v norme“, no pacient sa cíti zle</em></a>: when the results are “normal” and the patient still feels unwell. It starts from <a href="https://www.medscape.com/viewarticle/longevity-boom-how-respond-when-normal-isnt-enough-2026a10012h5">Julie Peck’s report on Medscape</a> of 14 October 2026. I take the views of eight clinicians in the longevity and wellness segment as prompts, not as proof. The tables, limits, and references are on that site. Here is the gap that a demand for more tests easily covers over.</p>
<p><a href="https://doi.org/10.2337/dc21-0723">Alfego and colleagues</a>, in Diabetes Care (2021), analysed 28,295,982 adults with diabetes or hypertension in a U.S. national laboratory database, 2013 to 2019. Guideline-concordant kidney assessment, meaning both eGFR and UACR in the same year, was missing in 80.3%. An eGFR was present in 89.6%. A urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio was present in 21.0%. A complete assessment at least once reached 28.7% of patients with diabetes, 10.5% with hypertension, and 41.4% with both. Annual testing rates ranged from 5% to 30% across states, and the national rate rose from 10.7% to 15.2% between 2013 and 2018. The authors recall the estimate that about 37 million Americans have chronic kidney disease and that nearly 90% of them do not know it. These are U.S. data. A comparable Slovak figure for UACR coverage, at the same resolution, is not publicly available.</p>
<p>The sentence “your results are normal” collapses three different things in clinic. The value fell inside a reference interval, which is usually built to cover the middle 95% of a healthy population. With ten mutually independent analytes, the chance that at least one falls outside is about 40% (1 − 0.95<sup>10</sup>). A value inside the interval does not say whether this person has changed from their own baseline. Second meaning: a diagnostic threshold was not crossed. A threshold is a convention. Third: the test answered the question it was ordered for.</p>
<p>For the kidneys, that distinction decides something. Serum creatinine depends on muscle mass, protein intake, and age. In a sarcopenic or older patient it can stay inside the reference interval while filtration is substantially reduced. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.kint.2023.10.018">KDIGO 2024</a> therefore recommends confirming eGFR with cystatin C where the accuracy of the estimate changes management, and staging chronic kidney disease by cause, eGFR category, and albuminuria category. A negative dipstick for protein does not reliably catch moderately increased albuminuria. An eGFR of 90&nbsp;ml/min/1.73&nbsp;m² or higher does not exclude albuminuria category A2 or A3.</p>
<p><a href="https://doi.org/10.1161/HYPERTENSIONAHA.121.17323">Shin and colleagues</a>, in an individual-participant meta-analysis from the CKD Prognosis Consortium (Hypertension, 2021), showed that albuminuria in patients with hypertension and diabetes carries prognostic information that filtration does not replace. Leaving it out systematically underestimates risk.</p>
<p>Demand for longevity care is growing faster than the evidence for it. Future Market Insights estimates the global market for longevity-clinic chain infrastructure at 2.6 billion USD in 2026 and 6.9 billion a decade later. That is a commercial estimate cited in the Medscape report, not a peer-reviewed figure, and it reads as a marker of demand. Behind it is the experience that report collects: after a routine blood draw, the answer is that the laboratory and vital signs are normal, while sleep, mood, weight, libido, and stamina stay as they were.</p>
<p>The criticism of excess testing belongs in that debate. The systematic review by <a href="https://doi.org/10.1186/s13012-015-0356-4">Cadogan and colleagues</a> (Implementation Science, 2015) reports that about 30% of primary-care contacts end in a laboratory request, and that a substantial share of those requests does not help care. Of eleven studies, four randomised, nearly all reduced test volume after an intervention. The effect size ran from 1.2% to 60%. The authors themselves call the evidence low-level and the quality weak. Imaging is sharper. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj.k2387">O’Sullivan and colleagues</a> (BMJ, 2018), in an umbrella review, showed that the prevalence of incidental findings varies by method from under 5% to more than a third of examinations, and that most incidentalomas do not lead to a clinically important diagnosis.</p>
<p>For whole-body MRI in asymptomatic people, the pooled prevalence of critical and indeterminate incidental findings was 32.1% (95% confidence interval 18.3 to 50.1), and the pooled proportion of false-positive findings was 16.0% with a very wide interval, 1.9 to 65.8 (<a href="https://doi.org/10.1002/jmri.26736">Kwee and Kwee</a>, 2019). None of the included studies followed verification of negative findings beyond five years. The meta-analysis by <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s00330-025-11976-5">Martins da Fonseca and colleagues</a> (European Radiology, 2026) found confirmed malignancy in 1.57% (95% confidence interval 1.22 to 2.03) of 9,024 asymptomatic participants across ten studies. Most studies had a moderate to serious risk of bias. Low yield, frequent incidental findings, and missing long-term outcomes still limit clinical use. For a nephrologist the consequence is concrete. The cascade after an indeterminate finding often continues with computed tomography using iodinated contrast, or with a biopsy. When eGFR is reduced, a test with a low chance of benefit adds a real risk.</p>
<p>The same holds for part of what is offered without an indication. A medically indicated infusion is ordinary practice. The problem is administration without an indication: vascular access, infection, volume and sodium in a person with chronic kidney disease or heart failure. Oxalate nephropathy after high-dose intravenous vitamin C is documented. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s40620-024-02078-4">Zou and colleagues</a> (Journal of Nephrology, 2025) describe acute kidney injury after a short series of doses in a patient with multiple myeloma. Vitamin C is metabolised to oxalic acid. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1053/j.ajkd.2020.03.030">Leonberg-Yoo and colleagues</a> (American Journal of Kidney Diseases, 2020) note that dietary supplements are especially common in people who consider themselves healthy, including living kidney donors. Signals of possible nephrotoxicity from natural products in FDA reports generate a hypothesis. They do not prove cause (<a href="https://doi.org/10.1177/27683605261456017">Lukan and colleagues</a>, 2026). <a href="https://doi.org/10.1097/MD.0000000000028997">Kwon and colleagues</a> (Medicine, 2022) describe oxalate nephropathy after long-term use of chaga, the fungus Inonotus obliquus, presenting as nephrotic syndrome.</p>
<p>For dehydroepiandrosterone, evidence of benefit is weak. A <a href="https://doi.org/10.1002/14651858.CD006221">Cochrane review</a> from 2006, in people over 50 without dementia, found no support for a favourable effect of supplementation on cognition. In one study, visual memory was worse than with placebo. The meta-analysis by <a href="https://doi.org/10.1080/09513590.2019.1616175">Lin and colleagues</a> (Gynecological Endocrinology, 2019) suggested a partial increase in hip and trochanter density in women, not in men. The authors themselves called the clinical importance unproven, and the direction of the reported effects is inconsistent in the paper, so I do not take a magnitude from it. For pregnenolone, in the indication of slowing ageing, there is no comparable base of evidence. Absence of evidence of benefit is not evidence of no effect. For an expensive intervention with an unclear indication and no defined measure of success, the burden of proof sits with the person who offers it.</p>
<p>The American Heart Association statement (<a href="https://doi.org/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001184">Ndumele and colleagues</a>, Circulation, 2023) names cardiovascular-kidney-metabolic syndrome as a condition arising from the link between metabolic risk, chronic kidney disease, and the heart and vessels. It proposes stages 0 to 4. It treats fragmented care as an obstacle. An HbA1c from prevention, chemistry from the emergency department, and a lipid panel from cardiology can each be in order, and a shared picture over time can still be missing. What needs adding is albuminuria and the slope of eGFR.</p>
<p>In diabetes, hypertension, obesity, cardiovascular disease, or a family history, the UACR is usually the test that is missing. Expanding panels across the board increases the number of findings. It does not increase health.</p>
<p><em>This is a commentary on a review for clinicians, not a treatment decision for an individual patient, and not a substitute for the KDIGO recommendations, a summary of product characteristics, or national guidance.</em></p>
